Remove the stale Output hold-out from DrakeParseTest's definition gate

Dev accepted, in these words: "Accepted, build it" — the recommendation being to remove `inlineTupleArgument` from
DrakeParseTest: the val and its comment, the `filterNot` on the `parse(emit(td))` walk and the file-only "mods held out
of the JSON gate" log line, so terrestrial Output enters the definition gate and every definition in the corpus with a
surface is under both round-trip gates with no exception list. No engine, definition or Scala change.

Why. The hold-out was added at 0acf2da on 2026-08-12 with a comment that was true then: a tuple argument carrying an
application — Output's `("type", io.circe.Json.fromString("LOCATION"))` — rendered inline, an inline `( … )` group was
one token to the lexer, and the application inside came back as host-opaque text instead of the `()` tree it went out
as, so `parse(emit(td))` could not reproduce Output's JSON while the surface itself round-tripped. Two dated changes
made it false. The parser trees tuple and collection literals since 2026-09-20 and splits a glued token at depth-zero
parentheses, commas and dots since 2026-09-16, so the group is read, and Output's payload value is a full tree in the
committed JSON: an application of `io.circe.Json.obj` over four tuples, each holding an application. And since
c5b8089 on 2026-10-29, when Output's message became a case branch, Output.json has been `DrakeCLI parse` of its own
surface, so the definition is the parser's reading by construction. The widened scope of a4ff2c4 kept the hold-out
unchanged and its record said so; it was the one definition of 73 outside the definition gate, and the only entry the
set ever held.

MEASURED before recommending, on the compiled classes of the a4ff2c4 direct run: `DrakeCLI parse Output.drake` is the
committed Output.json byte for byte, and `DrakeCLI check` over the terrestrial directory reports surface drift 0, json
drift 0; a scratch copy of the suite with the set emptied, compiled into the overlay and run focused: 381 tests, zero
failed, Output's `parse(emit(td))` case green, surface losses 0 across 183 types, parse scope 110 + 73. A grep of the
corpus finds no other inline tuple argument carrying an application, and the records name no other hold-out.

Built as measured. The three sites go; the report test keeps its other three file-only lines and the console headline.
Flagged with the recommendation, not recommended: keeping the set as an empty mechanism against a future hold-out —
dead code guarding a case the parser no longer has; a future exception should be argued when it appears.

